data registry_census_note: NonEmptyStr = "KNOWN INCOMPLETE, declared: the running srv1-4 hosts, their boards, PSUs, drives, NICs and the fastener/cable bins are not yet rows - the physical census that lands with the intake workflow adds them. A row absent from the registry is untracked capital, which is exactly the state this module exists to eliminate, so the census obligation is the registry's own first pressure case."

fn registry_ids_resolve() -> Bool {
  fold(asset_registry, init: true, f: fn(acc, h) {
    acc && count(filter(asset_classes, a => a.id == h.asset_id)) == 1
  })
}

data liquidation_note: NonEmptyStr = "WHY THE EXIT SIDE IS MODELED THIS HARD (operator 2026-08-30: 'companies never handle that process well'): the failure mode is not one bad sale, it is the ABSENCE OF A PROCESS - assets idle without an owner or a deadline, depreciate to zero, then leave as untyped shrinkage or a dumpster run, with data-bearing media as the compliance time bomb in the pile. Every mechanism here is the negation of one piece of that: DecisionForced negates open-ended idling; the typed ExitChannel with per-channel proceeds negates the unmeasured recovery rate; ledger conservation negates silent shrinkage; the sanitization wall negates the drive that walks out with data on it; HarvestedIntoParts closes the loop back into inventory (a dead server is a parts feed for the fleet - the decompose move applied to hardware). The disposition ledger also feeds the same intake instruments in reverse: what we learn selling (real recovery rates per channel) reprices what we are willing to pay buying - the vintage frontier's exit value stops being a guess. NOT MODELED YET, declared: per-unit serial ledgers (this module is class-grain; unit grain lands with the intake census), tax treatment of disposals, and the actual sale workflow (listing, escrow, shipping) which arrives as ordinary workflow modeling when the first real liquidation happens."
